WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.532
Four objects.

00:00:01.572 --> 00:00:03.768
So why does
furniture take ‘is’?

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:07.439
A chair, a table,
a sofa and a bed.

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:08.705
I can count them:

00:00:08.745 --> 00:00:10.755
one chair,
two chairs.

00:00:10.795 --> 00:00:12.622
But furniture
is the overall label

00:00:12.662 --> 00:00:14.144
for the whole
collection,

00:00:14.184 --> 00:00:16.245
not the name
of each item.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.359
English treats that
word as uncountable.

00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:21.833
So: ‘The furniture
is expensive.’

00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:22.808
Not ‘are’.

00:00:23.737 --> 00:00:24.674
Need a number?

00:00:24.714 --> 00:00:25.925
Count pieces:

00:00:25.965 --> 00:00:28.726
‘Two pieces of furniture
are expensive.’

00:00:28.766 --> 00:00:30.459
The number
goes on pieces,

00:00:30.499 --> 00:00:32.271
so the verb
is plural.

00:00:33.203 --> 00:00:35.044
Same picture
with luggage:

00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:36.325
‘The luggage
is heavy’,

00:00:36.365 --> 00:00:38.771
but ‘three pieces
of luggage are heavy’.

00:00:38.811 --> 00:00:40.468
See what
the noun names,

00:00:40.508 --> 00:00:42.121
then choose
the verb.

